The library `libnvdsgst_sparse4d.so` is a custom DeepStream video-template
implementation for Sparse4D multi-camera 3D perception. It performs real-time
3D object detection, tracking, and multi-view fusion across camera streams
using temporal and spatial modeling.

It is loaded by `gst-nvdsvideotemplate` through `customlib-name` (not a
standalone GStreamer plugin). `bash build/build.sh` builds and installs it as
part of the gst-plugins stage after `scripts/install_opensource_deps.sh` runs
`configs/install_dependencies.sh`.

Refer to the DeepStream SDK documentation for more details.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Pre-requisites:
- NVIDIA DeepStream SDK 9.2 (helpers, meta, nvbufsurface, nvdspreprocess)
- CUDA runtime (`CUDA_VER=13.4` for x86/SBSA CUDA13.4.3;
  `CUDA_VER=13.2` for Jetson CUDA13.2.2)
- TensorRT development headers/libraries: 11.4.0.106 on x86 and generic SBSA,
  11.4.1.31 on JetPack7.2.2 Jetson; these are separate candidate stacks
- PyTorch 2.9.0+cu130 at `/usr/local/lib/python3/dist-packages/torch`
  (override with `TORCH_PATH` if installed elsewhere). It must independently
  identify as ABI1 and contain genuine CUDA libraries for the target architecture.
- libeigen3-dev, nlohmann-json3-dev, libyaml-cpp-dev
- GStreamer 1.0 development packages
- Camera calibration files for the target dataset

`scripts/install_opensource_deps.sh` installs the apt packages and PyTorch by
running `configs/install_dependencies.sh`. To install those deps only:

  $ sudo bash configs/install_dependencies.sh

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Compiling and installing the library:

  $ export CUDA_VER=13.4 # x86 and SBSA. Jetson uses CUDA_VER=13.2
  $ make && sudo -E make install

Skip sudo when already root (for example inside a container).

On ARM, pass `DEEPSTREAM_VARIANT=sbsa`, `DEEPSTREAM_VARIANT=tegra`, or
`DEEPSTREAM_VARIANT=igx` explicitly when needed. The default recognizes IGX
from `/proc/device-tree/model`, then Jetson from `/etc/nv_tegra_release`;
containers without those host markers must specify their variant. IGX keeps
its installed legacy stack: when `TRT_VER` is unset or empty, its version is
read from the selected TensorRT development headers rather than pinned to 11.4.
This preserves input selection only, not a new IGX/TRT10 qualification. The selected
TensorRT header version must match `TRT_VER`; override `TRT_INCLUDE_DIR` and
`TRT_LIB_DIR` for a non-system installation. ELF architecture and SONAME checks
do not independently prove the runtime's exact patch version or compatibility.

For example, a Jetson source rebuild uses:

  $ make CUDA_VER=13.2 DEEPSTREAM_VARIANT=tegra

The default build directory separates architecture/CUDA/TensorRT selections,
including both the objects and linked custom library.
Set `BUILD_DIR` to a fresh directory after changing Torch or other dependency
inputs; do not interpret a timestamp-only no-op as a rebuilt stack. Builds use
`-O3`, dependency files and local-header prerequisites. Overrides do not make
ABI0 Torch, another architecture's libraries or missing target headers valid.

These checks cover build inputs, not Sparse4D model/MSDA-plugin compatibility,
accuracy, latency or memory qualification. Rebuild engines for the chosen stack
and validate real models on the target. This custom-library build does not
rebuild or enable the separate OCR libraries.

The library libnvdsgst_sparse4d.so is installed at
/opt/nvidia/deepstream/deepstream/lib/

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Features:
- Multi-camera 3D object detection and tracking
- Support for TensorRT optimization (FP16/FP32)
- Camera calibration and projection matrix handling
- Temporal instance tracking with feedback mechanism
- Integration with DeepStream analytics pipeline
- Support for various input formats (RTSP, file, etc.)
- Instance re-identification across frames
- Birds-Eye-View (BEV) perception
- Real-time performance optimizations

## Configuration

### Main Configuration File (config.yaml)
The plugin uses a YAML configuration file that controls all aspects of the Sparse4D pipeline:

#### Inference Properties
- `onnx_file`: Path to the Sparse4D ONNX model
- `engine_file`: Path for TensorRT engine serialization
- `labels_file`: Path to class labels file
- `gpu_id`: GPU ID to use for tensor operations
- `batch_size`: Sparse4D model batch size
- `num_sensors`: Number of sensors/cameras
- `enable_fp16`: Enable FP16 precision for inference
- `force_engine_rebuild`: Force rebuilding of TensorRT engine
- `feedback`: Enable feedback mechanism for temporal tracking

#### Calibration Properties
- `calib_file_path`: Path to camera calibration file
- `bev_group_name`: BEV group name in calibration file
- `calib_mode`: Calibration mode (synthetic, etc.)
- `use_camera_groups`: Group cameras for multi-view fusion
- `recentering`: Recalculate camera coordinates from reference point

#### Preprocessing Properties
- `preprocessed_height`: Height of preprocessed images
- `preprocessed_width`: Width of preprocessed images
- `aug_configs`: Image augmentation configuration

#### Instance Bank Properties
- `num_anchor`: Number of anchors for instance bank
- `embed_dims`: Embedding dimensions
- `anchor`: Path to anchor file
- `anchor_handler`: Handler class for anchor processing
- `num_temp_instances`: Maximum temporary instances
- `default_time_interval`: Default time interval for temporal projection
- `confidence_decay`: Confidence decay for tracked instances
- `max_time_interval`: Maximum time interval for tracking

#### Decoder Properties
- `num_output`: Maximum number of output bounding boxes
- `object_confidence_threshold`: Confidence threshold for filtering

#### Visualisation Properties
- `generate_3d_bbox`: Generate 3D bounding boxes for display
- `bbox_color_mode`: Color mode for bounding boxes
- `display_class_labels`: Display class labels
- `display_instance_ids`: Display instance IDs
- `display_class_score`: Display class score

#### Debugging Properties
- `silent`: Suppress perf logs
- `display_tensor_info`: Display tensor information
- `display_bbox_list_info`: Display bboxlist information
- `max_objects_to_display`: Maximum number of objects to display in bboxlist
- `dump_frames`: Enable frame dumping for debugging
- `dump_max_frames`: Number of frames to dump
- `gpu_postprocess`: Perform postprocessing on GPU

### DeepStream Integration

Add the following to your DeepStream application config to use the plugin:

```
[primary-gie]
enable=1
plugin-type=2
config-file=videotemplate_custom_lib_config.txt
```

Configure the plugin in `videotemplate_custom_lib_config.txt`:

```
gpu-id=0
customlib-name=/opt/nvidia/deepstream/deepstream-9.2/lib/libnvdsgst_sparse4d.so
```

## Example Pipeline

The DeepStream pipeline for Sparse4D processing typically includes:
1. Source elements (File/RTSP/HTTP)
2. Streammux
3. Video conversion and preprocessing
4. ds-sparse4d plugin for 3D detection and tracking
5. On-screen display for Visualisation
6. Output (display/file/message broker)

Example pipeline configurations are provided in the `configs` directory:
- `deepstream_test5_app_sparse4d.txt`: Basic pipeline with MEGA configuration
- `deepstream_test5_app_sparse4d_filesrc.txt`: File source configuration
- `deepstream_test5_app_sparse4d_realtime.txt`: Real-time streaming configuration

## Usage Examples

### Sparse4D Setup
The `sparse4d_setup.sh` script automates the setup process for the Sparse4D plugin. It handles the following tasks:

1. Configures the environment based on your systems GPU architecture
2. Copies and validates configuration files
3. Checks for required model files (ONNX, labels)
4. Validates the camera calibration file
5. Either uses a pre-built TensorRT engine or generates one from your ONNX model

You can customize the setup by setting these environment variables before running the script:
- `SPARSE4D_CONFIG_PATH`: Custom path for configuration files
- `ONNX_MODEL_PATH`: Custom path to ONNX model
- `MODEL_LABELS_PATH`: Custom path to labels file

To run the setup script:
```bash
bash configs/sparse4d_setup.sh
```

### Run with DeepStream Test App
To run the plugin with a file source-based pipeline:
```bash
deepstream-test5-app -c configs/deepstream_test5_app_sparse4d_filesrc.txt
```

## Common Scenarios

### Scenario 1: Changing Number of Input Streams

When changing the number of input streams/cameras, you must update the batch size settings in multiple configuration files to ensure consistency. This is critical for proper operation of the Sparse4D pipeline.

#### Required Configuration Updates:

1. **config.yaml** (Sparse4D config):
   ```yaml
   # Update the number of sensors to match your stream count
   num_sensors: 12  # Change this to your desired number of streams
   ```

2. **deepstream_test5_app_sparse4d.txt** (DeepStream pipeline config):
   ```
   [source-list]
   # In case of filesrc config, set num-source-bins to match your camera count
   num-source-bins=12

   # Update max-batch-size to match the number of streams
   max-batch-size=12  # Change this to your desired number of streams

   [streammux]
   # Update batch-size (though this is typically ignored when using source-list)
   batch-size=12  # Change this to your desired number of streams

   [tiled-display]
   # IF display is enabled, update rows and columns to match your camera count
   rows=3
   columns=4
   ```

3. **config_preprocess.txt** (Preprocessing config):
   ```
   # Update network-input-shape 1st value in the preprocess configuration
   network-input-shape= 12;3;512;1408  # Change this to your desired number of streams
   ```

### Scenario 2: Changing the Sparse4D Model

The Sparse4D plugin supports model swapping, allowing you to use different Sparse4D models based on your specific use case. Follow these steps to change the model:

#### 1. Update the Configuration File

Modify the `config.yaml` file with the following parameters:

```yaml
# Model configuration
onnx_file: /path/to/new/sparse4d/model.onnx  # Path to your new ONNX model
labels_file: /path/to/labels.txt             # Update if the new model has different classes
force_engine_rebuild: True                   # Force TensorRT engine rebuild for the new model
```

#### 2. Model Compatibility Requirements

Ensure your new model meets these requirements:
- Uses the same input tensor names and shapes as expected by the plugin
- Has compatible output format with the post-processing logic
- Follows the Sparse4D architecture pattern for 3D object detection

### Scenario 3: Component-wise Performance Measurement

To enable detailed performance measurements for each component in the Sparse4D pipeline, set the `silent` parameter to `false` in your `config.yaml` file:

```yaml
# Debugging properties
silent: False  # Set to False to enable performance measurements
```

With performance measurement enabled, you will see detailed timing information for each processing stage in the pipeline. Example output:

```
ProcessBuffer Frame-668 │ ⚡ Starting
   │    │
   │    ├─► PreProcess │ ⚡ Starting
   │    └─ Found NVDS_PREPROCESS_BATCH_META
   │    └─ Tensor Shape: 1x12x3x512x1408
   │    └─ Tensor Name: img
   │    └─ Updated TensorRT binding for input tensor[img] with preprocessed buffer
   │    ├─► PreProcess │ ⏱    0.04ms │ ✔ Completed
   │    │
   │    ├─► TensorRT Inference │ ⚡ Starting
   │    ├─► TensorRT Inference │ ⏱   25.41ms │ ✔ Completed
   │    │
   │    ├─► Postprocessing Tensors │ ⚡ Starting
   │    │    │
   │    │    ├─► Cloning Inference Output Tensors to CPU │ ⚡ Starting
   │    │    ├─► Cloning Inference Output Tensors to CPU │ ⏱    0.47ms │ ✔ Completed
   │    │    │
   │    │    ├─► Instance Bank │ ⚡ Starting
   │    │    └─ Updating Instance Bank
   │    │    └─ Caching Instance Bank
   │    │    └─ Getting Instance ID
   │    │    └─ Getting Feedback tensors from instance bank
   │    │    └─ Updating Feedback Tensor: Interval Mask Tensor
   │    │    └─ Updating Feedback Tensor: Input Cached Anchor
   │    │    └─ Updating Feedback Tensor: Input Cached Feature
   │    │    ├─► Instance Bank │ ⏱    1.06ms │ ✔ Completed
   │    │    │
   │    │    ├─► Decoding Tensors │ ⚡ Starting
   │    │    └─ Found 1 decoded batch with 299 detections
   │    │    ├─► Decoding Tensors │ ⏱    0.58ms │ ✔ Completed
   │    │    │
   │    │    ├─► Attaching bbox3dList to batch_meta as a user meta │ ⚡ Starting
   │    │    ├─► Attaching bbox3dList to batch_meta as a user meta │ ⏱    0.23ms │ ✔ Completed
   │    │    │
   │    ├─► Postprocessing Tensors │ ⏱    2.43ms │ ✔ Completed
   │    │
ProcessBuffer Frame-668 │ ⏱   27.95ms │ ✔ Completed
```

This output provides detailed timing information for each component:
- PreProcess: 0.04ms
- TensorRT Inference: 25.41ms
- Postprocessing: 2.43ms
  - Cloning Inference Output Tensors: 0.47ms
  - Instance Bank processing: 1.06ms
  - Decoding Tensors: 0.58ms
  - Attaching metadata: 0.23ms
- Total processing time: 27.95ms

### Scenario 4: Enabling BBox Detection Display

To visualize the detection results from the Sparse4D plugin, you need to configure both the Sparse4D Visualisation properties and the DeepStream display components. This allows you to see the detected objects with bounding boxes directly on the video feed.

#### 1. Enable Visualisation Properties in config.yaml

Update the following Visualisation properties in your `config.yaml` file:

```yaml
# Visualisation properties
generate_3d_bbox: True          # Generate 3D bounding boxes for display
bbox_color_mode: class_id       # Color mode: "class_id" or "instance_id"
display_class_labels: True      # Display class labels
display_instance_ids: True      # Display instance IDs
```

#### 2. Configure DeepStream Display Components

Update your DeepStream application config file (e.g., `deepstream_test5_app_sparse4d_filesrc.txt`) to enable Visualisation components:

```
# Enable tiled-display for multi-camera view
[tiled-display]
enable=1
rows=3                # Adjust based on your camera count
columns=4             # Adjust based on your camera count
width=1920            # Display resolution width
height=1080           # Display resolution height
gpu-id=0
nvbuf-memory-type=0

# Enable on-screen display for bounding boxes
[osd]
enable=1
gpu-id=0
border-width=2
text-size=15
text-color=1;1;1;1;   # White text (RGBA)
text-bg-color=0.3;0.3;0.3;0.7   # Semi-transparent background
font=Arial
show-clock=0
clock-x-offset=800
clock-y-offset=820
clock-text-size=12
clock-color=1;0;0;0
nvbuf-memory-type=0

# Enable display output (EglSink)
[sink0]
enable=1
type=2                # 2=EglSink for display output
sync=0
source-id=0
gpu-id=0
nvbuf-memory-type=0
```
